#heading(level: 3, outlined: false)[Contents]

#toc()

= Preface

What follows was set twice, in two implementations, to find out which of its
rules were about type and which were about one engine.

= Composition

The page is the first decision and the typeface is very nearly the last. What
follows is an argument for taking them in that order.

== The measure of a line

Between 45 and 75 characters, and 66 at the centre of the range. Past 75 the
return sweep fails; below 45 the eye fixates too often for the sentence to hold
together.

== Justification and its costs

A clean right edge is bought with uneven word spacing, and the currency is the
hyphenation dictionary. Take both or neither.

== Opening a chapter: the drop cap and the small-caps lede

A drop cap, small caps on the opening words, and nothing else. The signal is
that something has begun, and one signal is enough.

= Tabular matter

Rules, not grids. Three horizontal rules and no vertical ones, because the
columns already read as columns.

== Figures and their captions

The caption goes below the figure, carries a small-caps label, and never leaves
the page its figure is on.

= Apparatus

Footnotes where the engine can set them, endnotes where it cannot, and sidenotes
wherever the page can afford the margin.

== References

Hanging indent, always. The surname is the thing being scanned, so it must be
the leftmost thing on the entry.
